-- title:
--   §3.2, proof of Prop. 6 — consecutive iterates of (19)–(21): $\|x_{k+1}-x_k\|\le 2\alpha_k c$
-- statement:
--   Consider problem (5)–(6) under the standing assumptions, and let $\{x_k\}$ be a sequence generated by iteration (19), (20) or (21) with the cyclic order and positive stepsizes constant within each cycle, under Assumption 1 (for (19), (20)) or Assumption 2 (for (21)) with constant $c$. Then for every $k\ge1$,
--   $$\|x_{k+1}-x_k\|\le 2\alpha_kc.$$
--
--   With $\alpha_k\to0$ this gives $\|x_{k+1}-x_k\|\to0$, which the proof of Proposition 6 uses to pass from convergence of the cycle-start iterates $x_{km}$ to convergence of the whole sequence.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper writes $\|x_{k+1}-x_k\|\le2\alpha_kc$ for all $k$. The bound uses $x_k\in X$ (to compare with $P_X(x_k)=x_k$), which holds for $k\ge1$ because every step ends with a projection, but not for an arbitrary starting point: with $X=\{0\}$, $x_0=5$, all components zero and $c=0$, $\|x_1-x_0\|=5$. We therefore state it for $k\ge1$. Only the norm bounds (22)/(24) are used.
-- source:
--   Bertsekas, Incremental Proximal Methods for Large Scale Convex Optimization, LIDS-P-2847 (rev. March 2011), §3.2, proof of Proposition 6, p. 15

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_IncProx_Cyclic_Basic

namespace IncProx.Cyclic

/-- §3.2, proof of Proposition 6 (p. 15). For a sequence generated by iteration (19), (20) or (21)
with the cyclic order under Assumption 1 or 2 with constant `c`, consecutive iterates satisfy
‖x_{k+1} − x_k‖ ≤ 2α_k c for every k ≥ 1. -/
theorem iterate_step_bound {n m : ℕ} [NeZero m] (P : Problem n m) (hP : P.Standing) (A : Alg)
    (α : ℕ → ℝ) (hα : CyclicStepsize m α) (x z gF gH : ℕ → Vec n)
    (hrun : IsRun P A cyc α x z gF gH) (c : ℝ) (hA : AssumptionFor P A x z gF gH c) :
    ∀ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k → ‖x (k + 1) - x k‖ ≤ 2 * α k * c := by sorry

end IncProx.Cyclic
